  <section id="intro">
    <h2>Why rivers are good for wildlife watching</h2>
    <p>
      A river is a corridor. It links uplands to the sea, and many animals follow it to feed, breed or travel. Good river wildlife spotting is mostly about patience and timing: dawn and dusk, quiet banks, and a little knowledge of what to look for. This guide covers species you have a realistic chance of seeing, with honest notes on how hard each is. It is a general introduction rather than a field guide, so pair it with a proper identification book or app.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="birds">
    <h2>Birds</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Kingfisher.</strong> Often seen only as a flash of electric blue low over the water. Look along quiet, clear stretches with overhanging perches, and listen for a sharp whistle.</li>
      <li><strong>Grey heron.</strong> Stands motionless in the shallows, or flies with slow wingbeats and a folded neck. Easy to see along most lowland rivers.</li>
      <li><strong>Dipper.</strong> A small, round bird that bobs on rocks in fast upland streams and walks underwater to feed. A good sign of clean, fast water in the north and west.</li>
      <li><strong>Grey wagtail and sand martins.</strong> Wagtails flick their tails on stones; sand martins nest in earth banks in summer and skim insects over the water.</li>
      <li><strong>Swans, ducks and grebes.</strong> Widespread, and a good way to start. Keep your distance from nests and young.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="mammals">
    <h2>Mammals</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Otter.</strong> Otters have recovered across much of the UK as water quality has improved, but they are shy and mostly active at night. You are more likely to see signs, such as footprints and droppings on rocks, than the animal. Early morning on quiet stretches gives the best chance.</li>
      <li><strong>Water vole.</strong> A small, round-faced vegetarian, often mistaken for a rat, which has declined sharply in the UK and is legally protected. Listen for a "plop" into the water and look for neat feeding remains and burrows in the bank.</li>
      <li><strong>Bats.</strong> Several species hunt over rivers at dusk. A bat detector or a quiet evening by a bridge is enough to start.</li>
      <li><strong>Beavers.</strong> They have been reintroduced in some areas, including parts of Scotland and England. Look for gnawed stumps and dams, but only where they are known to be present.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="fish">
    <h2>Fish</h2>
    <p>
      Clear rivers let you watch fish from the bank. Brown trout and grayling prefer clean, cool water; chub, roach and dace shoal in slower stretches; and pike lurk in weedy margins. Atlantic salmon are the most celebrated, returning from the sea to spawn in the river where they were born, usually from autumn into winter. In autumn, some rivers have a good place to watch salmon leap at weirs and falls, but disturbing spawning fish and their redds (gravel nests) is harmful, so keep out of the water and stay on paths. Fishing rules and seasons vary by river; see <a href="/blog/scotland-salmon-fishing-packages/">our Scottish salmon guide</a> and <a href="/blog/chalk-stream-fly-fishing-day-tickets/">our chalk stream guide</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="insects">
    <h2>Insects and plants</h2>
    <p>
      Damselflies and dragonflies patrol riverbanks in summer, mayflies hatch in spring and early summer, and caddis flies, stoneflies and freshwater shrimps live in the gravel. Their presence is a good indicator of water quality. Water crowfoot, a white-flowered plant, carpets chalk streams in early summer, and banks are lined with willow, alder, reeds and meadowsweet. Watch out for invasive plants such as Himalayan balsam and giant hogweed, which can harm native species or your skin.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="watching">
    <h2>Watching responsibly</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Stay on paths and out of the water in breeding and spawning seasons.</li>
      <li>Keep dogs under control. They disturb nesting birds and water voles, and can pick up pollution.</li>
      <li>Do not touch or move nests, eggs or young animals. Some species are legally protected.</li>
      <li>Move slowly, keep voices low, and wear muted colours.</li>
      <li>Report pollution incidents, dead fish or sewage to the Environment Agency, Natural Resources Wales or SEPA.</li>
      <li>Clean, drain and dry your boots and kit between rivers to avoid spreading invasive species.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="where">
    <h2>Where to start</h2>
    <p>
      Every <a href="/rivers/">river guide</a> on this site lists the wildlife people commonly associate with that river, and short notes on where to walk. Rivers like the <a href="/rivers/river-tweed/">Tweed</a>, <a href="/rivers/river-spey/">Spey</a> and <a href="/rivers/river-itchen/">Itchen</a> are well known for wildlife, and a walk along any quiet river at dawn is a good place to begin. If you plan to paddle, read <a href="/blog/river-kayaking-safety-for-beginners/">our beginner safety guide</a> first.
    </p>
  </section>

